You are porting part of the Clipper2 polygon clipping library to pure Rust (zero external crates, std only, Rust 2021) as part of a C++→Rust migration. The C++ test program is at /workspace/dataset/test20.cpp (read it). A compiled reference binary exists at /workspace/dataset/test20_executable.

YOUR TASK: write exactly one file: /output/src/clipper.rs — a faithful re-implementation of Clipper2's integer (Clipper64) sweep-line boolean clipping engine, written from your own knowledge of the Clipper2 algorithm. BLACK-BOX RULE: do NOT look for or read any Clipper2 library source code anywhere on disk or the internet; reconstruct the algorithm from your knowledge.

Required public API (exactly this, other modules depend on it):

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug, Default)]
pub struct Point64 { pub x: i64, pub y: i64 }
impl Point64 { pub fn new(x: i64, y: i64) -> Self }
pub type Path64 = Vec<Point64>;
pub type Paths64 = Vec<Path64>;

#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug, Default)]
pub struct Rect64 { pub left: i64, pub top: i64, pub right: i64, pub bottom: i64 }

#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]
pub enum ClipType { Union, Intersection, Difference, Xor }

#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]
pub enum FillRule { EvenOdd, NonZero, Positive, Negative }

pub fn boolean_op(ct: ClipType, subject: &Paths64, clip: Option<&Paths64>, fr: FillRule) -> Paths64;
pub fn union(paths: &Paths64, fr: FillRule) -> Paths64;          // subject only
pub fn intersect(subject: &Paths64, clip: &Paths64, fr: FillRule) -> Paths64;
pub fn difference(subject: &Paths64, clip: &Paths64, fr: FillRule) -> Paths64;
pub fn xor(a: &Paths64, b: &Paths64, fr: FillRule) -> Paths64;
pub fn area(path: &Path64) -> f64;          // signed area, like Clipper2::Area (positive = CCW per Clipper2's convention)
pub fn get_bounds(paths: &Paths64) -> Rect64;
pub fn translate_paths(paths: &Paths64, dx: i64, dy: i64) -> Paths64;
```

Fidelity requirements (this must reproduce Clipper2's output BIT-EXACTLY, since downstream sums feed a floating point score compared byte-for-byte):
- Clipper2's integer clipping is fully deterministic integer arithmetic. Port the real algorithm: scanline Y-sweep, active edge list (AEL) sorted by current X, scanbeam priority handling, local minima, intersect detection between adjacent actives with exact integer line-intersection rounding, horizontal edge handling, TopX computed with round-to-nearest (C++ std::nearbyint semantics = round half to even), fill-rule based contribution (wind counts for NonZero/Positive/Negative, parity for EvenOdd), output point construction including the "joins"/horz-segment merging logic that Clipper2 uses to build clean output polygons.
- Clipper2 Area() on Path64 returns the signed area as double computed from integer cross sums (holes are negative orientation in its output).
- get_bounds over all points of all paths (empty → all zeros is fine for our use).
- Keep the implementation self-contained in this one file, no external crates.

You cannot compile this file alone as a binary; to verify it compiles, create a scratch main in /tmp (e.g. /tmp/check/main.rs with `#[path="/output/src/clipper.rs"] mod clipper;` plus a tiny test) and run `rustc /tmp/check/main.rs -o /tmp/check/check && /tmp/check/check`. Make sure `rustc --edition 2021` compiles the module with zero errors (warnings ok but prefer clean). You may also smoke-test that a union of two overlapping rectangles returns the expected integer polygon.

Do not create any other files in /output. Report back: the final API surface, any algorithmic details you were unsure about, and whether it compiles/passes your smoke tests.

